# The one position a lent C pointer may stand in, asked at EVERY position
# in the file (design.md §4.20; spec § FFI; defect 022, found by the
# ffi-pragmatist at panel 121).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE. The question is about a POSITION, not about a
# type in hand: `check/builtins.hero` types `s.cstr()` and answers `cstr`,
# which is right and says nothing about where that answer may go. That
# file is also at its decided ceiling with no room. The shape here is
# `check/ffi_sweep.hero`'s, and for that module's own reason: a pass over
# the whole file rather than a question at one call site.
#
# WHAT IT REFUSES, and the count that first justified it was wrong three
# ways. `spec § 13 FFI` says `.cstr()` lends a `str` to C to read **for that
# call**, and panel 122 turned that into three clauses: a lend stands only
# as an argument of a call (R2), no function outside an `extern` group
# answers `cstr` (R3), and no record outside a group holds one (R4).
# Measured after the sitting: **0 of 638** files in this tree change
# verdict, so the whole rule costs this corpus nothing.
#
# The first version of this doc claimed 67 of 67 `.cstr()` sites were
# already arguments of an extern call. It was 66; two of the four
# re-reads were not externs at all — `bind.system(...)` is the NEGATIVE
# fixture `tests/golden/surface-fixtures/externroute/wrong.hero` and
# `as_text.say(...)` is a Heroes wrapper — and the blast radius that came
# with it undercounted sites threefold. A grep line was read where a file
# had to be opened, three times running.
#
# IT ASKS THE VALUE AND NOT THE WORLD (CLAUDE.md §11). The legal position
# is built by reading the call nodes in hand, so a position the AST gains
# tomorrow is refused by default rather than admitted in silence. That is
# what closes `xs.push(s.cstr())` and `Rec(field: s.cstr())`, which the
# first draft of this rule would have allowed: both are argument
# positions, and only an `extern` call is one that reads the bytes and
# returns.
#
# THE EXTERN DISCRIMINATOR IS BACK SINCE PANEL 171, and it was gone for a
# reason worth keeping. Panel 122 removed it on three grounds: two qualified
# calls that look identical differ in legality by what another file declares
# (the ergonomist's veto), no language uses which KIND of function is called
# as a lifetime criterion (the historian found none), and it refused three
# SOUND sites in the wrapper §4.19 prescribes as the route out of a module.
# The author's ruling of 2026-09-20 spends all three: under the pessimistic
# default the legality of EVERY lend depends on what the declaration says
# (`lent`), a wrapper's own `cstr` parameter is an unmarked one so the lend
# stops at it either way, and the historian found D's druntime writing the
# mark in exactly this shape. So a lend reaches a `lent` parameter of an
# `extern` function or nowhere, and `check/lend_landing.hero` asks it.
#
# THE LEASE IS `check/leasing.hero`'S, since M-held-bytes (panel 124, ratified
# 2026-09-09; panel 125). It is §4.19's FOURTH case, the mirror of the lend,
# and it borrows this file's two instruments — `is_builtin_named` and
# `argument_of` — because its rule is the lend's position rule applied to a
# cell. It was written into this file first, which put it at 366 lines in
# `suite_layout.hero`'s unit against §11's 300, and the suite said so; the
# seam it went out along is the one this paragraph names.
#
# WHY THE LITERAL CASE GOES TOO. `return "lit".cstr()` is sound today,
# because `HERO_STR_STATIC` gives a literal static storage and an
# immortal refcount, while `return ("a" + n.to_str()).cstr()` is a
# use-after-free that builds with zero diagnostics and prints an empty
# line at exit 0. The two are one brace apart and what separates them is
# invisible at the call site, so the rule refuses both rather than
# teaching a reader to tell them apart (author decision 2026-09-09,
# choosing the strict rule over the escape-only one).

use ast
use source
use parse
use resolve
use check/lower
use resolved
use inventory
use check/state
use check/lend_landing
use check/lend_decls
use lend_errors
use field_lend_errors

# Whether this expression is a lend: `s.cstr()` or `cstr(s)`. The
# RESOLVER answers, not the text — a name is only a builtin because the
# resolver said so, and `check/builtins.hero`'s own dispatch reads the
# same table.
function is_lend(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, at: i64) -> bool
    return is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "cstr") || is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")

function is_builtin_named(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, at: i64, name: str) -> bool
    named = match a.exprs[at].kind
        .method                                                                     => at
        .call cl                                                                    => cl.callee
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit  => 0 - 1
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index        => 0 - 1
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => 0 - 1

    if named < 0
        return false

    match r.uses[named]
        .builtin b                            => return inventory.table()[b.at].name == name
        .top | .local | .module | .unresolved => return false

# The call this expression is an argument of, for every expression in the
# file. A UFCS receiver IS the first argument (§4.11), which is what makes
# `"p".cstr().hero_file_read(@status)` legal.
#
# THE MATCH IS IN A FUNCTION OF ITS OWN so that the arms which record
# nothing can be a `return`. Written inline in the `while` below, they
# were a `continue` — which the spec forbids for exactly this reason,
# § Control flow: *"An arm that does nothing is a block holding `_ = 0` —
# `continue` is not one"*. In a `while` it jumps past the increment, and
# the sweep hung on its first run.
function record_arguments(a: ast.Ast, at: i64, @into: {i64: i64})
    match a.exprs[at].kind
        .call cl =>
            for arg in cl.args
                into[arg.value] @ at
        .method m =>
            into[m.receiver] @ at

            for arg in m.args
                into[arg.value] @ at
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit  => return
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index        => return
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return

function argument_of(a: ast.Ast) -> {i64: i64}
    into: {i64: i64} @ {}
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < a.exprs.len()
        record_arguments(a, at, @into)
        at @ at + 1

    return into

# **A RECORD'S CONSTRUCTION IS THE ONE CALL WHOSE ARGUMENT OUTLIVES IT** (defect
# 067, panel 167's completeness critic, 2026-09-20). The position rule above
# blesses an argument position because a call READS the bytes and returns;
# `Box(p: x)` does not return, it KEEPS — the pointer is a field of a value the
# statement hands on, and `end_lease` or the frame's end then leaves it dangling.
#
# BOTH DIRECTIONS REACH IT AND THE DEFECT WAS FILED AS ONE. A lease name:
# `end_lease(@x)` empties the cell and the copy inside the record does not move,
# so C reads freed bytes at exit 0. A lend: `return Box(p: s.cstr())` parks a
# pointer into a `str` that dies with the frame — `check` exit 0, the honest 72
# read back as 0, `heap-use-after-free` under the sanitizer. This module's own
# doc claims it closes `Rec(field: s.cstr())`, and it does — for a record
# OUTSIDE a group, through `no_cstr_in_a_record`. Inside a group that clause
# steps aside on a premise that is true of a header's field and false of a lend:
# *there C owns the bytes*.
#
# THE FOUR SHAPES BESIDE IT ARE ALREADY REFUSED, measured before this clause was
# written: a variant case, an array literal, a map literal and `ok(x)` each
# refuse a lease name today. The record constructor was the one door, which is
# why the rule names that shape rather than widening to every construction.
#
# IT ASKS THE VALUE AND NOT THE WORLD: the callee's own declaration is read, so
# a construction form the AST gains tomorrow is refused by default rather than
# admitted in silence.
function constructed_record(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, decls: [ast.Decl], call: i64) -> i64?
    named = match a.exprs[call].kind
        .call cl                                                                    => cl.callee
        .method                                                                     => 0 - 1
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit  => 0 - 1
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index        => 0 - 1
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => 0 - 1

    if named < 0
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a call")

    match r.uses[named]
        .top t =>
            match decls[t.at].kind
                .record_decl => return ok(t.at)
                .function_decl | .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => return fail(
                    code: "none",
                    msg: "not a record"
                )
        .builtin | .local | .module | .unresolved => return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a declared name")

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "unreached")

# The clause as the two sweeps ask it: a construction that KEEPS, and whose
# record is not already condemned by its own fields.
function keeps_its_argument(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, decls: [ast.Decl], into: {i64: i64}, at: i64) -> bool
    if into[at].is_err()
        return false
    built = constructed_record(a, r, decls, call: into[at].must())

    if built.is_err()
        return false
    return !lend_decls.holds_a_refused_field(c, decls, built.must())

# The sweep. A lend may stand only as an argument of a call (panel 122 R2),
# and since panel 171 only as an argument of an `extern` function whose
# parameter says `lent` — the module doc above carries why the discriminator
# left and why it is back.
function lent_only_into_c(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    lend_decls.no_cstr_out_of_heroes(@c, a, s, decls)
    lend_decls.no_cstr_in_a_record(@c, a, s, decls)

    # The early exit panel 122 R8 asks for: 637 of 638 files in this tree hold
    # no lend at all, and building a parent map over every expression of every
    # one of them cost 1.3% of `heroes check` for nothing.
    if !holds_a_lend(a, r)
        return
    into = argument_of(a)
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < a.exprs.len()
        # A record's construction is an argument position that KEEPS (defect
        # 067), so a `cstr` lend standing there escapes exactly as one standing
        # nowhere does, and gets the same code: the class is one — a pointer
        # outliving the bytes — and a second name for one question is a panel
        # path (`ffi_parameter_position`'s own reasoning, one module over).
        #
        # **AND IT IS THE `cstr` LEND'S ALONE, measured rather than assumed.** A
        # `ptr` lend stands only as an argument of an EXTERN call since panel
        # 166's clause landed, and a record's constructor is never one — so the
        # header clause below already refuses every construction. Applying this
        # one too put two messages on one span, which is the thing it exists to
        # avoid: `10:19 field_lend_escapes` and `10:19 field_lend_needs_a_header`
        # for the same mistake.
        kept = keeps_its_argument(@c, a, r, decls, into: into, at: at)

        if into[at].is_err() || kept
            if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "cstr")
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, lend_errors.cstr_escapes(a.exprs[at].span))

        if into[at].is_err()
            if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, field_lend_errors.field_lend_escapes(a.exprs[at].span))

        # **AND A LENT FIELD MUST NAME A PLACE** (panel 164). The lend hands C
        # the field where it lies so C may write back into it, and that is only
        # true when the bytes belong to a binding the program can still see:
        # `make().nsap.ptr()` lends a returned temporary, and C's write lands in
        # storage the next statement may reuse. Measured before this clause
        # existed: it reached the emitter, found no place to render, and aborted
        # at run time saying `this is a compiler bug`.
        #
        # It asks the RECEIVER's own shape and nothing about the world around it
        # (`.claude/rules/module-shape.md`): a chain of fields rooted at a name.
        if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")
            if !lends_a_place(a, at)
                state.push_diagnostic(@c, field_lend_errors.field_lend_needs_a_place(a.exprs[at].span))

        # **AND EVERY LEND LANDS ON A HEADER'S PARAMETER THAT SAYS IT LETS GO**
        # (panel 166 for the field, panel 171 and the author's ruling of
        # 2026-09-20 for both). Three questions, asked in `check/lend_landing.hero`
        # in the order a reader meets them, stopping at the first that fails
        # so one lend gets one message. The HEADER: only a function of an
        # `extern` group can carry a mark, and only a header says whether C
        # writes through the pointer — a Heroes function taking `ptr` has a
        # plain `void *`, and one taking `cstr` forwards to an unmarked
        # parameter either way. The EXTENT, for a field's address: `f(p: ptr,
        # n: i64)` relates its two parameters not at all, so an overstated
        # number read and WROTE past the field at exit 0 (defect 063). The
        # WORD: an unmarked C parameter is assumed to KEEP what it is handed,
        # and a lend lives for its call alone (defects 066 and 068). Absence is
        # a refusal and never silence, for all three.
        #
        # A `cstr` lend standing in a record's construction is `cstr_escapes`'s
        # above, or the field rule's when the record is condemned, and is not
        # asked again; a `ptr` lend there IS asked, because a constructor is
        # never an extern call and the header clause is the message that fits
        # it (the tests say why, one each).
        if !into[at].is_err() && is_lend(a, r, at)
            if is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr") || constructed_record(a, r, decls, call: into[at].must()).is_err()
                lend_landing.landed_on_a_header(
                    @c,
                    a,
                    r,
                    s,
                    decls,
                    call: into[at].must(),
                    at: at,
                    field: is_builtin_named(a, r, at, name: "ptr")
                )
        at @ at + 1

# Whether a `ptr` lend's receiver is a place: `s.name`, `s.inner.name`, and
# never a call's result, a literal or an index. The root is a NAME, which is the
# one shape whose storage outlives the call the lend stands in.
function lends_a_place(a: ast.Ast, at: i64) -> bool
    match a.exprs[at].kind
        .method m => return rooted_at_a_name(a, m.receiver)
        .call cl => return cl.args.len() == 1 && rooted_at_a_name(a, cl.args[0].value)
        .name | .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit => return false
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field | .field_name | .index => return false
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return false

function rooted_at_a_name(a: ast.Ast, at: i64) -> bool
    match a.exprs[at].kind
        .field fl => return rooted_at_a_name(a, fl.base)
        .name => return true
        .int_lit | .char_lit | .float_lit | .str_lit | .interp | .bool_lit | .call => return false
        .null_ptr | .hole | .unary | .binary | .field_name | .index | .method => return false
        .variant_case | .array | .map | .try_expr | .if_expr | .match_expr | .error => return false

function holds_a_lend(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved) -> bool
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < a.exprs.len()
        if is_lend(a, r, at)
            return true
        at @ at + 1

    return false

# Every clause fires, and the legal shape stays quiet. `check/ffi_sweep.hero`'s
# own test is the model, and the lowering loop is not decoration: R3 and R4
# read `written_types`, which `lower_ty` is what fills.
function swept(text: str) -> i64
    p = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(p.tree, s, p.decls, p.uses)
    c: state.Checker @ state.new_checker(out_of: out, expr_count: p.tree.exprs.len())
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < p.tree.types.len()
        _ = lower.lower_ty(@c, p.tree, out, i)
        i @ i + 1

    lent_only_into_c(@c, p.tree, out, s, p.decls)
    return c.out.diagnostics.len()

test "a lend bound instead of passed is refused"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    c = x.cstr()\n    return n(s: c)\n") == 1

test "a Heroes function may not answer cstr"
    assert swept("function f(x: str) -> cstr\n    return x.cstr()\n") == 2

test "a record outside a group may not hold a cstr"
    assert swept("record H\n    c: cstr\n\nfunction f(h: H) -> H\n    return h\n") == 1

test "the legal shape stays quiet: a lend as an argument of a `lent` parameter, and a group's own record"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record C\n        name: cstr\n    function n(s: cstr lent) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    return n(s: x.cstr())\n") == 0

# Panel 171: the default is the pessimistic one. A lend at an unmarked `cstr`
# parameter is refused; a lease name, `nullptr`, a `cstr` C handed back and any
# name are admitted there, because none of them is bytes that die with the
# call — and the pointer C hands back is the shape the sitting wrote down as
# what the flip leaves standing.
test "a lend at a parameter not marked lent is refused, and what is not a lend stays quiet"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    return n(s: x.cstr())\n") == 1
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    l: cstr @ x.lease()\n    k = n(s: l)\n    end_lease(@l)\n    return k\n") == 0
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f() -> u64\n    return n(s: nullptr)\n") == 0
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n    function g() -> cstr\n\nfunction f() -> u64\n    return n(s: g())\n") == 0

# And a `cstr` lend handed to a function of this program stops there: the
# wrapper cannot carry the mark, so the lend is made inside it instead, which
# is the shape §4.19 prescribes and the one panel 122 removed the clause for.
test "a cstr lend handed to a function of this program is refused, and the wrapper that lends inside itself stays quiet"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr lent) -> u64\n\nfunction wrap(s: cstr) -> u64\n    return n(s: s)\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    return wrap(s: x.cstr())\n") == 1
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    function n(s: cstr lent) -> u64\n\nfunction wrap(x: str) -> u64\n    return n(s: x.cstr())\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    return wrap(x: x)\n") == 0

# Panel 164's two clauses, each made to fire and each shown quiet on the shape
# beside it. The legal case is third on purpose: a rule with no quiet case is a
# rule nobody can tell from "refuse everything".
test "a lent field kept in a binding is refused"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    k: ptr @ s.b.ptr()\n    return f(p: k, n: 4)\n") == 1

test "a lent field of a value nothing holds is refused"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n lent, n: i64) -> i64\n    function make() -> S\n\nfunction g() -> i64\n    return f(p: make().b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 1

# Panel 171's half for the field: the marked parameter is quiet, the unmarked
# one is refused, and an uncounted one gets the EXTENT message alone, whose
# note names both words — one message per lend, whichever word is missing.
test "a lent field passed straight into C stays quiet, and is refused once where a word is missing"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n lent, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 0
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 1
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 1

# A field reached through another field is still a place, which is the shape
# beside the one that provoked the rule (CL-061).
# Defect 067: a group's record CONSTRUCTION is an argument position that keeps,
# and the four shapes beside it were run before this clause was written — a
# variant case, an array literal, a map literal and `ok(x)` each refuse already.
test "a lend parked in a group's record is refused, and the call that reads it stays quiet"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record Box\n        p: cstr\n    function n(s: cstr) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> Box\n    return Box(p: x.cstr())\n") == 1
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record Box\n        p: cstr\n    function n(s: cstr lent) -> u64\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> u64\n    return n(s: x.cstr())\n") == 0

# A record of THIS program is refused by the FIELD rule, and the construction
# clause steps aside there: one mistake gets one message, and the second
# disappears the moment the first is fixed (defect 062's direction).
test "a lend parked in a record of this program gets ONE message, the field's"
    assert swept("record Box\n    p: cstr\n\nfunction f(x: str) -> Box\n    return Box(p: x.cstr())\n") == 1

# And a record whose fields are LEGAL is refused too, which is what says the
# stepping-aside is about a CONDEMNED record and not about whose record it is.
# The `ptr` lend gets the header clause's message rather than this one, because
# a constructor is never an extern call — measured, and the reason the clause
# above is the `cstr` lend's alone.
test "a lend parked in a record with legal fields is refused, by the clause that fits it"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nrecord Box\n    p: ptr\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> Box\n    return Box(p: s.b.ptr())\n") == 1

test "a lent field nested one record deep is still a place"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record I\n        b: u8[4]\n    record S\n        i: I\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n lent, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.i.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 0

# Panel 166's clause: the callee must be the group's. A wrapper taking `ptr`
# and a function value are the two ways a lend used to reach C with no header
# asked; the wrapper that takes the record is the legal shape beside them.
test "a lent field handed to a function of this program is refused, through a name and through a value"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction wrap(p: ptr, n: i64) -> i64\n    return f(p: p, n: n)\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return wrap(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n") == 1
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction wrap(p: ptr, n: i64) -> i64\n    return f(p: p, n: n)\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    h = wrap\n    return h(s.b.ptr(), 4)\n") == 1

test "a wrapper that takes the record and lends inside itself stays quiet"
    assert swept("extern \"s.h\"\n    record S\n        b: u8[4]\n    function f(p: ptr counted_by n lent, n: i64) -> i64\n\nfunction total(s: S) -> i64\n    return f(p: s.b.ptr(), n: 4)\n\nfunction g(s: S) -> i64\n    return total(s: s)\n") == 0
